[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-29", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 29 — 99.5% of the index sets are singletons at 160 bytes each; full SF1 would fit at ~4.5 GB", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-29.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 29 - 99.5% of the index's sets hold one value, and each costs 160 bytes for being a set\n\n> Seed: iteration 28's S19' - count the index nodes before proposing a\n> representation. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2 (no row; the measurement\n> M3 needed).\n\n## Generate\n\n  Walk the subset db's four indexes: inner maps, sets, elements,\n  singleton sets. Then price a singleton set: one million `{k #{v}}`\n  entries against one million `{k v}`, live heap after gc.\n\n  Prediction: most (entity, attribute) pairs are single-valued in this\n  data (an id, a date, a creator), so eavt/aevt singletons dominate;\n  avet less so (many entities share a value).\n\n## Rank (simeon, 2,004,832 quads)\n\n    index   inner maps   sets       elements   singletons   share\n    eavt       502,357   1,652,749  2,004,832  1,643,941    0.995\n    aevt            23   1,652,749  2,004,832  1,643,941    0.995\n    avet            23     438,655  2,004,832    193,999    0.442\n    vaet             0           0          0            0      -   (no ref-valued quads)\n\n    a map entry whose value is a singleton PersistentHashSet   211 B\n    the same entry holding the value directly                   51 B\n    overhead of being a set                                    160 B\n\n  The prize: (1.64M + 1.64M + 0.19M) x 160 B = ~557 MB of the subset's\n  ~830 MB. Scaled by full SF1's counts: ~6.4 GB of its 10.9 GB used heap.\n  Full SF1 with singletons held directly: ~4.5 GB -- resident on the\n  16 GiB fleet host with room; SF3 (~13 GB) within a 32 GB one.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Held, and sharper than predicted: not \"most\" but 99.5%. The datom\n    model's set-valued attribute is the right semantics and the wrong\n    default representation for a graph whose attributes are almost all\n    cardinality-one.\n  - This is a representation change inside datalog.index: hold the\n    counterpart directly while there is one, promote to a set on the\n    second, and let the read seam (`entity-attrs`, `by-predicate`,\n    `by-predicate-value`) present sets as before -- or present the bare\n    value and move the three readers. Either way it is an engine\n    decision with a measured prize, and the workspace's rule is that\n    such decisions are ADRs: proposed as root ADR-2609121100, with this\n    iteration's numbers as the evidence and the equivalence suites\n    (datalog 127 tests, kotobase-peer 262, the 1,082-parameter gate) as\n    the acceptance.\n  - What the loop can do next without the decision: nothing on M3. What\n    it can do with it: the SF1-proper row on the existing host.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict: MEASURED, no row. M3's first step has a design with a number\nattached (6.4 GB of 10.9), awaiting the decision.\n\nSeeds for iteration 30, in order:\n\n  - S20 (on decision): implement the singleton representation in\n    datalog.index behind the read seam; acceptance = the three suites\n    and the gate; then the SF1-proper row from levi.\n  - S18 (host) remains the alternative if the decision is no.\n  - S15 (coverage), M2 (updates).\n"}]
